1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Wrappers let primitives enter object
collections. That solves storage.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:15,160
It does not solve a different kind of lie:
a collection that claims to hold
"anything." Picture an old-style list of
names: Look at this java for a moment. The
cast is a confession.

3
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:29,320
You are telling the compiler, "trust me."
Runtime is where trust breaks. So the
natural question is: can the collection
itself remember what it holds, so the
mistake dies at compile time? That is what
generics are for.

4
00:00:29,439 --> 00:00:41,479
A type parameter says what belongs inside.
Generics move class-cast failures to
compile time — if you learn the rules,
including wildcards. Look at this java for
a moment.

5
00:00:41,599 --> 00:00:55,439
List<String> rejects names.add(42) before
the program runs. get returns String
without a cast. The type argument is part
of the API contract, not a comment. Using
raw types throws that safety away.

6
00:00:55,559 --> 00:01:08,359
But there is a twist learners must hear
early: type parameters erase at runtime.
The compiler uses <String> for checking,
then much of that detail is removed for
the JVM's older object model.

7
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:20,439
At runtime you largely see a list of
objects with inserted checks. That is why
you cannot write List<int> — no primitive
type arguments — and why wrappers from the
previous episode matter for List<Integer>.

8
00:01:20,559 --> 00:01:35,559
Erasure is the compatibility deal Java
made. Once you write APIs that accept or
return collections of related types,
wildcards appear. The mnemonic is PECS:
producer extends, consumer super.

9
00:01:35,679 --> 00:01:47,799
Look at this java for a moment. If a list
produces values for you to read, ? extends
Number is safe — every element is some
kind of Number. If a list consumes values
you want to put in, ?

10
00:01:47,979 --> 00:01:51,099
super Number is safe — it can accept a
Number.

11
00:01:51,219 --> 00:02:04,579
Wrong wildcard bounds are not a style
debate; they are a symptom of mixing "I
need to read" with "I need to write."
Generic methods solve a related need:
sometimes the type parameter belongs to
the method, not the class.

12
00:02:04,699 --> 00:02:18,379
Look at this java for a moment. Here <T>
is declared on the method. Call sites can
pass a List<String> or a List<Order> and
get the matching element type back. The
compiler infers T from the argument.

13
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:31,780
What if we skip generics and live with raw
types? Look at this java for a moment. It
feels flexible. Then every read needs a
cast, and unchecked warnings start
lighting up. Those warnings are clues, not
noise.

14
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:44,420
An unchecked warning means the compiler
lost the proof it normally gives you.
Ignoring them is how ClassCastExceptions
hide until a customer hits the bad path.
Let's make the generic contract visible in
a small helper.

15
00:02:44,539 --> 00:02:56,659
Look at this java for a moment.
Box<String> and Box<Integer> share the
same bytecode-shaped class after erasure,
but at compile time they are different
contracts. You cannot set(42) on name.

16
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:08,259
That is the everyday value of generics:
one implementation, many safe uses.
Untyped lists forced casts and late
failures. Generics restored compile-time
clarity.

17
00:03:08,379 --> 00:03:17,019
Erasure explained the runtime shape and
the ban on primitive type arguments. PECS
guided wildcards. Generic methods
localized type parameters.

18
00:03:17,139 --> 00:03:32,099
Notice how wrappers and generics cooperate
in real APIs: List<Integer>, Map<String,
Double>. The type parameter needs a
reference type, so the previous episode's
wrappers are how primitives enter generic
APIs.

19
00:03:32,219 --> 00:03:36,699
Now that types can carry parameters,
another kind of metadata wants a home:

20
00:03:36,819 --> 00:03:49,379
not "what type is this list," but "this
method overrides a parent," or "this field
is injected," or "this test is disabled."
How does Java attach structured notes that
tools and frameworks can read?

21
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:52,080
That is the doorway into annotations.
